Rule V1 (doc/scope_model.mld, section 3.5), with assumption D9 (section 2):
dune compiles every implementation of a virtual library against the one
interface of each virtual module. A driver documents that interface once in
the virtual library, and once more in each implementation, as an
implementation copy with the same name and digest. When the candidates for a
root are the virtual library's unit and marked copies of it, V1 picks the
copy if there is exactly one, and the virtual library's unit otherwise.

The setup is what dune builds for a virtual library [vlib] with one virtual
module [Clock], and two implementations [impl_a] and [impl_b]:

[vlib] has dune's alias module [Vlib], with [module Clock = Vlib__Clock].
The file [vlib/vlib.ml] stands for dune's [vlib.ml-gen]. [clock.mli] is
compiled to [vlib__Clock.cmi] with [-open Vlib].

[impl_a] has its own alias module [Vlib__impl_a__], and a private module
[Extra] compiled with [-open Vlib__impl_a__]. dune links [vlib]'s [.cmi]
files into [impl_a]'s directory; here [-I vlib] does the same job. dune also
compiles each implementation's [clock.ml] with [-cmi-file] pointing at
[vlib__Clock.cmi]. This test does not need those files; [v1_source.t] uses
them for source links.

[user] is built against [vlib] only. [tool] is built against [impl_a], and
so [vlib]. dune gives [impl_a] to it with [-H].

Every compile uses dune's [-no-alias-deps], and [-L] for the unit's own
library and its dependencies.

  $ cat impl_a/vlib__impl_a__.ml
  (* generated by dune *)
  
  (** @canonical Vlib.Clock *)
  module Clock = Vlib__Clock
  
  (** @canonical Vlib.Extra *)
  module Extra = Vlib__impl_a__Extra
  $ cat user/user.mli
  val stamp : unit -> Vlib.Clock.t
  (** See {!Vlib.Clock} and {!Vlib__Clock}. *)
  
  val direct : Vlib__Clock.t
  $ cat tool/tool.mli
  val stamp : unit -> Vlib.Clock.t
  
  val direct : Vlib__Clock.t
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -opaque -nopervasives -nostdlib -w -49 -I vlib -o vlib/vlib.cmo -c -impl vlib/vlib.ml
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -opaque -I vlib -open Vlib -o vlib/vlib__Clock.cmi -c -intf vlib/clock.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -opaque -nopervasives -nostdlib -w -49 -I impl_a -o impl_a/vlib__impl_a__.cmo -c -impl impl_a/vlib__impl_a__.ml
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -opaque -I impl_a -I vlib -open Vlib__impl_a__ -o impl_a/vlib__impl_a__Extra.cmi -c -intf impl_a/extra.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I user -I vlib -c user/user.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I tool -H impl_a -I vlib -c tool/tool.mli

The driver documents [vlib]'s [vlib__Clock.cmti] three times: once for
[vlib], and once for each implementation, with that implementation's
[--parent-id] and [-L] set. This is what odoc_driver does
([find_virtual_interface] in [src/driver/compile.ml]). odoc has no flag yet
to mark a copy (D9), so these copies are not marked.

  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/vlib -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib vlib/vlib__Clock.cmti
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/vlib -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib vlib/vlib.cmt
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/impl_a -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib vlib/vlib__Clock.cmti
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/impl_b -L impl_b:h/pkg/impl_b -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib vlib/vlib__Clock.cmti
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/impl_a -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib impl_a/vlib__impl_a__.cmt
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/impl_a -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib impl_a/vlib__impl_a__Extra.cmti
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/user -L user:h/pkg/user -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib user/user.cmti
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/tool -L tool:h/pkg/tool -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib tool/tool.cmti

How to read [odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents]. Each line
is one item of the signature. [x/0] is the item [x], with a number odoc adds
to tell apart items of the same name. [resolved(...)] is a resolved path, and
[root(pkg/vlib/Vlib)] is the unit [Vlib] of the library [vlib] in the
package [pkg]. [hidden(...)] marks a hidden unit, such as [Vlib__Clock],
which is never linked to (S3). [canonical(p,c)] is the path [p], shown as
its canonical target [c] (S1); [c] comes from a [@canonical] tag, and is
resolved at link.

V1.1. A unit built against the virtual library only.

[user]'s [stamp] has the type [Vlib.Clock.t]. Its root [Vlib] should mean
[vlib]'s [Vlib]. [Clock] is an alias written in [vlib]'s [Vlib], so its
target [Vlib__Clock] has the origin [Vlib], and should mean [vlib]'s unit.
[direct] names [Vlib__Clock] itself, with the origin [user]. [user]'s
libraries hold no copy, so it should mean [vlib]'s unit too.

  $ cat vlib/vlib.ml
  (* generated by dune *)
  
  (** @canonical Vlib.Clock *)
  module Clock = Vlib__Clock
  $ cat vlib/clock.mli
  type t
  
  val now : unit -> t
  $ odoc link -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a -L impl_b:h/pkg/impl_b -L user:h/pkg/user -L tool:h/pkg/tool h/pkg/user/user.odoc
  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/user/user.odocl
  val stamp/0 : resolved(unit) -> resolved(canonical(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock,resolved(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock)).t)
  val direct/1 : resolved(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)).t)

V1.2. A unit built against one implementation.

[tool]'s libraries are [tool], [impl_a] and [vlib]. [stamp]'s path goes
through [vlib]'s alias, as in V1.1, so [Vlib__Clock] there has the origin
[Vlib] and should still mean [vlib]'s unit. [direct]'s root has the origin
[tool]. Its candidates are [vlib]'s unit and [impl_a]'s copy. With the copy
marked (D9), V1 should pick [impl_a]'s copy.

odoc cannot mark the copy yet (D9). Until it can, V1.4 says what to expect,
and [direct] meaning either candidate would be wrong.

  $ odoc link -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a -L impl_b:h/pkg/impl_b -L user:h/pkg/user -L tool:h/pkg/tool h/pkg/tool/tool.odoc
  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/tool/tool.odocl
  val stamp/0 : resolved(unit) -> resolved(canonical(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock,resolved(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock)).t)
  val direct/1 : resolved(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)).t)

V1.3. A unit of the implementation itself.

[impl_a]'s [Extra] names [Vlib__Clock] directly, so the root has the origin
[Extra]. Its candidates are [vlib]'s unit and [impl_a]'s copy, as for
[tool]. With the copy marked (D9), it should mean [impl_a]'s copy. [Extra] is
hidden, so odoc does not link it, and the result at compile is the one that
stays.

Until odoc can mark the copy (D9), V1.4 applies here too, and [direct]
meaning either candidate would be wrong.

  $ cat impl_a/extra.mli
  val direct : Vlib__Clock.t
  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/impl_a/vlib__impl_a__Extra.odoc
  val direct/0 : resolved(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)).t)

V1.4. A copy that is not marked.

V1 applies only to marked copies. Today no copy is marked, so for [tool]'s
and [Extra]'s [direct] there are two candidates with one digest. The root
should be unresolved, with an ordinary warning (sections 3.5 and 3.6). The
answer should not depend on the order of the [-L] flags. Here the two units
are compiled again, with [vlib] given before [impl_a].

Picking either candidate would be wrong, and so would a result that changes
with the [-L] order.

  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/tool -L tool:h/pkg/tool -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a tool/tool.cmti
  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/tool/tool.odoc
  val stamp/0 : resolved(unit) -> resolved(canonical(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock,root(Vlib).Clock).t)
  val direct/1 : resolved(hidden(root(pkg/impl_a/Vlib__Clock)).t)
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/impl_a -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a impl_a/vlib__impl_a__Extra.cmti
  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/impl_a/vlib__impl_a__Extra.odoc
  val direct/0 : resolved(hidden(root(pkg/impl_a/Vlib__Clock)).t)

V1.6. References (R3 with V1, section 4.2).

How to read [odoc_print --refs]: each line is one reference in a doc
comment. [r(...)] is a resolved reference. [alias(p,r)] is a reference [r]
to an alias, whose target is the path [p].

[user]'s comment has [{!Vlib.Clock}] and [{!Vlib__Clock}]. R3 looks for
roots in the libraries given at link, here all of them. [Vlib] is only in
[vlib], so [{!Vlib.Clock}] should mean [vlib]'s [Vlib.Clock], an alias to
[vlib]'s [Vlib__Clock]. [{!Vlib__Clock}] has three candidates: [vlib]'s unit
and the two copies. With the copies marked, V1 should give [vlib]'s unit.
While they are not marked, it should be unresolved, with a warning (section
4.5). Either way, the [-L] order should not matter. Whether a reference to a
hidden unit should warn as well: the spec does not decide this yet (section
10).

While the copies are not marked, resolving [{!Vlib__Clock}] to any of the
three would be wrong.

  $ odoc_print --refs --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/user/user.odocl
  r(alias(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)),root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock))
  r(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)))
  $ odoc link -L impl_b:h/pkg/impl_b -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib -L user:h/pkg/user h/pkg/user/user.odoc
  $ odoc_print --refs --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/user/user.odocl
  r(alias(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)),root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock))
  r(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)))
  $ odoc link -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib -L impl_b:h/pkg/impl_b -L user:h/pkg/user h/pkg/user/user.odoc
  $ odoc_print --refs --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/user/user.odocl
  r(alias(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)),root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock))
  r(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)))

V1.5. The other units an implementation holds copies of (D9).

This case comes last, because it adds a unit to [impl_a].

dune also links [vlib]'s [vlib.cmi] into [impl_a]'s directory, with no
[.cmt]. D9 says such units are either not documented again, or marked as
copies. odoc_driver does not document them, because it finds no [.cmt] or [.cmti]. So
far, [Vlib] in [tool] had one candidate, and meant [vlib]'s [Vlib] (V1.2).

A driver that did document [impl_a]'s [vlib.cmi], without a mark, would
break D9. Then [tool]'s root [Vlib] has two candidates with one digest, and
should be unresolved, with an ordinary warning (section 3.5), in any [-L]
order.

Picking either candidate would be wrong. Picking [impl_a]'s [Vlib], which
has no doc comments, would lose the canonical tag too.

  $ cp vlib/vlib.cmi impl_a/vlib.cmi
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/impl_a -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib impl_a/vlib.cmi
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/tool -L tool:h/pkg/tool -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib tool/tool.cmti
  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/tool/tool.odoc
  val stamp/0 : resolved(unit) -> resolved(canonical(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib).Clock,root(Vlib).Clock).t)
  val direct/1 : resolved(hidden(root(pkg/vlib/Vlib__Clock)).t)
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/tool -L tool:h/pkg/tool -L vlib:h/pkg/vlib -L impl_a:h/pkg/impl_a tool/tool.cmti
  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/tool/tool.odoc
  val stamp/0 : resolved(unit) -> resolved(root(pkg/impl_a/Vlib).Clock.t)
  val direct/1 : resolved(hidden(root(pkg/impl_a/Vlib__Clock)).t)
